---
title: Email
description: The SMTP server that sends your transactional mail, and the templates for it.
section: Configuring a tenant
---

GateKeeper sends mail for exactly three things: **verifying an email address**, **resetting a
password**, and **delivering an MFA code**. All three are dead in the water without a working SMTP
provider, so this is usually the second thing you configure after your first client.

The **Email** page has two tabs: **Providers** and **Templates**.

## Providers

A provider is an SMTP server. Add one per tenant, or several if different applications should send
from different addresses.

| Field | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| **Name** | Your label for it. |
| **Host / Port** | The SMTP server. |
| **Username / Password** | Credentials. The password is encrypted at rest (AES-256-GCM) and never returned by the API. |
| **From email / From name** | What recipients see in their inbox. |
| **Encryption** | `starttls` (587), `tls` (465), or `none`. |
| **Default** | The provider used by any client that hasn't picked one. |

There are presets for **Gmail**, **Microsoft 365**, and **Hostinger**, which fill in the host, port,
and encryption for you.

> [!TIP]
> `none` exists for local development against a mail catcher like Mailhog. Never use it against a
> real SMTP server — the password crosses the network in the clear.

### Send a test email first

Every provider has a **Send test email** button. Use it. A wrong SMTP password does not surface as
a dashboard error at configuration time; it surfaces later as a user who never received their
password reset and has no way to tell you.

### Per-application providers

An OAuth client can be pinned to a specific provider, or left on **Tenant default**. This is how
one tenant sends its customer-facing mail from `hello@acme.com` and its internal admin mail from
`noreply@internal.acme.com`.

## Templates

Three templates, one per thing GateKeeper sends:

| Type | Sent when |
| --- | --- |
| `email_verification` | A new user needs to confirm their address. |
| `password_reset` | A user asked to reset their password. |
| `mfa_code` | A one-time code is being delivered by email. |

Bodies are written in **MJML** — a markup language that compiles to email-client-safe HTML, so you
do not have to hand-write nested tables to survive Outlook.

### Variables

Templates interpolate a small set of variables:

| Variable | Contains |
| --- | --- |
| `app_name` | The client's name. |
| `code` | The OTP, for `mfa_code`. |
| `link` | The action URL, for verification and reset. |
| `expiry` | How long the code or link is valid. |
| `support_email` | Your support address. |

Use the **live preview** while editing — it renders the MJML with sample values, so you can see
what lands in the inbox without sending yourself a dozen test mails.

> [!WARNING]
> A template that drops `{link}` from the password-reset mail, or `{code}` from the MFA mail,
> produces a perfectly valid email that the user cannot act on. The preview is there to catch
> exactly this; look at it before saving.

## What breaks without email

Worth being explicit, because the failures are all silent from the admin's side:

- **Self-service password reset** — the user gets no mail and cannot recover their account.
- **Email verification** — new users can never confirm, so they can never sign in.
- **Email MFA** — the code never arrives, and the user is locked out of an account that is
  otherwise fine.

If you have turned on self-registration or self-service password reset
([tenant settings](/docs/first-tenant)), you need a working provider.
